  score=4: LRC shell transporter
  score=3: Eisenstein D6 conjugation
  score=2: coimage sign-reversing involution
  score=1: raw complement merge
  score=0: raw scalar 7/21 match

Assumption Challenge
--------------------
Alternate vertices considered: tournament vertices, anti-maps,
rooted perspectives, residue shells, folded shell orbits, unit-distance
points, D6 symmetries, coimage cancellations, and proof obligations.
Chosen vertices: carrier lenses. They preserve the predicate
'Anti(X) is a transporter to JX and quotienting can hide it.'
They destroy exact embeddings, complete LRC certificates, and raw
Hamiltonian-path spectra beyond the checked SC range.
Challenged assumption: an anti-coset is special to SC tournaments.
It is the generic transporter shape behind complements, converses,
reflections, conjugations, and sign-reversing cancellations.

Interpretation
--------------
- SC tournaments give the clean theorem: Anti(T) is a coset over Aut(T).
- LRC shell work already uses the same object as transporters under
  doubling/reflection on Z/(2n-1); folding can erase reflection into Aut.
- Eisenstein/unit-distance finite point sets keep Anti as the orientation
  reversing D6 coset exactly at full centered shells and lose pieces on
  partial-shell prefixes.
- The practical next test is to replace every raw complement merge in the
  repo by an explicit transporter: name X, name J, compute Aut(X), compute
  Anti(X), and record what the quotient forgot.
